K3c diagnosis log — regular-image boot regression introduced by C4
====================================================================
Written by: K3c thread (Buffy), 2026-09-27
Status: NOT FIXED by this thread. Per AGENTS.md rules 7 and 11 ("one work
order at a time", "never edit code outside the work order to make a check
pass"), the K3c thread does not modify C4's bootchain integration. The
regression is C4's to fix. This file only proves attribution.

Summary
-------
The K3c FAT32 work order was complete and verified at 2026-09-26 16:35
(docs/logs/k3c-check-full.log: 4/4 QEMU PASS). The C4 thread then landed
five commits (e399866..284e4d6, 23:49-23:53 that evening) adding
blake2b bootchain verification to Limine config paths. Re-running
scripts/check.sh on HEAD (2026-09-27, after K3c commit 6d9bf04) shows:

  - FAIL (SeaBIOS/hdd) and FAIL (OVMF/hdd): the REGULAR image never
    reaches NOVA_BOOT_OK. Limine panics inside uri_resolve.
  - PASS (SeaBIOS/selftest) and PASS (OVMF/selftest): the selftest image
    (kernel/limine-selftest.conf, plain `path:` line) is unaffected.
  - check 5/9 collateral: "FAIL /LIMINE.CONF size 520 == build input 479"
    — +41 bytes = the 64-char sha256 hex minus the 23-char
    PLACEHOLDER_KERNEL_HASH token, i.e. the same substitution.

Root cause, from the vendored Limine source (external oracle)
-------------------------------------------------------------
C4 wrote (kernel/limine.conf, commit ab0d402):

    path: boot(1):/NUCLEUS#blake2b:PLACEHOLDER_KERNEL_HASH

and build-disk.sh (e399866) substitutes a 64-hex-char sha256 digest for
the placeholder. But vendored
.freebuff/ref/limine/limine-12.9.0/common/lib/uri.c (lines 65-90) parses
everything after `#` as the hash and panics unless it is exactly 128 hex
characters:

    // Get hash
    for (int i = (int)strlen(uri) - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
        if (uri[i] != '#') {
            continue;
        }
        uri[i++] = 0;
        if (hash != NULL) {
            *hash = uri + i;
        }
        if (strlen(uri + i) != 128) {
            panic(true, "Blake2b hash must be 128 characters long");
            return false;
        }
        // Validate all 128 characters are valid hexadecimal
        for (size_t j = 0; j < 128; j++) {
            char c = uri[i + j];
            if (!((c >= '0' && c <= '9') || (c >= 'a' && c <= 'f') || (c >= 'A' && c <= 'F'))) {
                panic(true, "Blake2b hash contains invalid character at position %d", (int)j);
                return false;
            }
        }

`blake2b:` + 64 sha256 chars is 72 characters — not 128 — so every load
of the regular kernel panics before the ELF is read. CONFIG.md (lines
435-437) confirms the intended syntax: `boot():/somemodule.tar#<128 hex
chars>` — no algorithm prefix. The substituted digest is also the wrong
algorithm: sha256 (64 hex) instead of blake2b over the file contents
(hashlib.blake2b(..., digest_size=64) is stdlib in Python if a generator
is wanted).

